*======================================================================
* amsinit.a99 - the memory card found, sized and tested (the loader only: the
* kernel
* keeps pager.a99's mapping routines)
*======================================================================

* HOW THE LOADER FINDS OUT WHAT MEMORY CARD IT HAS.  Nothing announces a SAMS
* card, and it cannot be asked by reading a register back: the 74LS612 mapper
* of the 4M board (Tekumel's TEK-024) has no path that returns a register to
* the bus (its low data pins are tied to ground and to the latch's outputs),
* and a proposal for an FPGA machine says the same of real cards, which
* "do not reliably allow" it.  Before 1.37.0 AMSDET wrote a probe to a register
* and read it back, and would have refused a perfectly good card.  What a
* mapper does that nothing else does is show a different piece of memory
* through a window when its register changes, so that is what is looked for:
* AMSINI writes the identity map (writes only), and AMSSIZ then writes
* markers into pages that are 1, 2, 4 ... pages apart and sees which of
* them are the same page.  Without a card every page is the same memory.

* WHY THE FIRST THING IS AN IDENTITY MAP.  Before mapping is switched on, the
* card passes the CPU's addresses straight through (pass-through mode).  When
* mapping is switched on (SBO 1) each 4K window shows the page its register
* names; if the registers are first set so that window n shows page n, nothing
* visibly changes: the kernel keeps running at the same addresses, and only
* afterwards does the loader start pointing windows at other pages.  Doing it
* in that order is what makes it safe to switch on a machine that is running
* from the very memory it is remapping.
* AMSINI - identity-map all 16 windows and switch mapping mode on.
* After this, page n is at address n<<12 for n = 0..15, which means
* the kernel at >A000 is on pages >A->D and nothing moved.
* Uses R1-R3, R12.
AMSINI LI   R12,AMSCRU         CRU base for the mapper card
       SBO  0                  registers visible
       CLR  R1                 window 0
       LI   R3,AMSREG          register for window 0
AMSI1  MOV  R1,R2              identity: window n gets page n, in the high
       SLA  R2,8               byte only (see MAPPG): with mapping off the
* card puts page n in window n, so this code runs on
       MOV   R2,*R3+
       INC  R1
       CI   R1,16
       JL   AMSI1
       SBO  1                  mapping mode on
       SBZ  0                  hide registers (they share DSR space)
       RT

* CURINI - record what the identity map put in each window (the tracking
* words live in slot A, so this runs after the loader has cleared it).
CURINI LI   R0,PGLOWA
       MOV  R0,@CURA2
       INC  R0
       MOV  R0,@CURA3
       LI   R0,PGLOWB
       MOV  R0,@CURBE
       INC  R0
       MOV  R0,@CURBF
       RT

* PAGEA - map pages R0 and R0+1 into slot A (>2000, >3000).
PAGEA  .enter
       MOV  R0,@CURA2          remember what slot A holds
       MOV  R0,R7              second page = first + 1
       INC  R7
       MOV  R7,@CURA3
       LI   R1,2               window >2000
       BL   @MAPPG
       MOV  R7,R0
       LI   R1,3               window >3000
       BL   @MAPPG
       .leave
       RT

* HOW TO COUNT PAGES WITHOUT ASKING.  A card with 256 pages decodes eight
* page-number bits; a page number with more bits just wraps around, so page
* >10 + 256 is the same page as >10.  AMSSIZ writes a marker into page >10,
* then writes a different marker into the page 1 above it, and looks to see
* whether page >10 still has its own marker.  If it does not, the two are the
* same memory: there is no mapper.  If it does, it doubles the distance and
* tries again; the first distance at which the write lands on page >10 is the
* number of pages the card has (2, 4 ... 32, 64, 128 ... up to 4,096, a
* 16-megabyte card: 1,024 is the 4M board, 2,048 the PicoPEB's 8M).
* AMSSIZ - how many pages the card has: R0 = 0 for no mapper (every page is
* the same memory), else 2, 4 ... 4096 (a 16M card).  Writes a marker to page
* >10 and checks whether a write to page >10+1, +2, +4 ... +2048 lands on it
* (the card decodes only as many page bits as it has).  Run after AMSINI and
* before anything is loaded: it overwrites the first word of page >10 and of
* the pages tried.  Leaf apart from MAPPG (R11 kept in R6).  Uses R0-R6, R12.
AMSSIZ MOV  R11,R6             MAPPG is called: keep the return address
       LI   R5,1               alias distance to try first
AZ1    LI   R0,>10             the reference page ...
       LI   R1,>E              ... into window >E000
       BL   @MAPPG
       LI   R4,>A55A
       MOV  R4,@>E000          its marker
       MOV  R5,R0              the page S above it
       AI   R0,>10
       LI   R1,>E
       BL   @MAPPG
       MOV  R5,@>E000          a different marker there
       LI   R0,>10             the reference page again
       LI   R1,>E
       BL   @MAPPG
       C    @>E000,R4          still its own marker?
       JNE  AZ2                no: page >10+S is page >10, so S pages exist
       SLA  R5,1               yes: try twice the distance
       CI   R5,4096
       JL   AZ1
AZ2    LI   R0,PGLOWB          window >E000 back to its own page
       LI   R1,>E
       BL   @MAPPG
       MOV  R5,R0              1 ... 2048, or 4096 when nothing aliased
       CI   R0,1
       JNE  AZ3
       CLR  R0                 neighbouring pages are one memory: no mapper
AZ3    B    *R6

* AMSTST - write a page-specific word to the first and last word of every
* page from >10 up to R0-1, then check them all.  Pages below >10 are
* skipped (slot A, the kernel's pages and slot B's default pair).  Run
* by the loader before loading: it is destructive.  Returns R0 = 0 if
* every page answered, else the first failing page.  R11 kept in R6;
* uses R0-R7, R12.
AMSTST MOV  R11,R6
       MOV  R0,R7              page limit
       LI   R5,>10             from page >10
AT1    MOV  R5,R0
       LI   R1,>E              window >E000
       BL   @MAPPG             the page there
       MOV  R5,R4              signature: the page number plus a constant (so
       AI   R4,>A55A           every page of 4096 has its own)
       MOV  R4,@>E000          at the page's first word ...
       MOV  R4,@>EFFE          ... and its last
       INC  R5                 the next page
       C    R5,R7              all of them?
       JL   AT1
       LI   R5,>10             then read them all back
AT2    MOV  R5,R0
       LI   R1,>E              window >E000
       BL   @MAPPG             the page there
       MOV  R5,R4
       AI   R4,>A55A
       C    @>E000,R4          its first word right?
       JNE  AT3
       C    @>EFFE,R4          and its last?
       JNE  AT3
       INC  R5                 the next page
       C    R5,R7              all of them?
       JL   AT2
       CLR  R5                 all answered
AT3    LI   R0,PGLOWB
       LI   R1,>E
       BL   @MAPPG
       MOV  R5,R0
       B    *R6
